WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.388 --> 00:00:01.396
Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.421 --> 00:00:04.911
Welcome to the Langfocus channel,
and my name is Paul.

00:00:04.936 --> 00:00:06.879
Today, we're going to
look at the differences

00:00:06.904 --> 00:00:10.943
between two languages that many
people mistakenly think are the same,

00:00:11.015 --> 00:00:12.695
Russian and Ukrainian.

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:16.601
The misconception that Russian
and Ukrainian are the same

00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:20.040
might stem from the fact that Russian
is widely spoken in Ukraine.

00:00:20.445 --> 00:00:25.277
The numbers differ depending on the survey,
but according to one 2015 survey

00:00:25.302 --> 00:00:29.096
60% of Ukrainians consider
Ukrainian their native language,

00:00:29.374 --> 00:00:32.358
15% consider Russian to
be their native language

00:00:32.466 --> 00:00:35.596
and 22% consider both to
be their native languages.

00:00:35.844 --> 00:00:39.604
These numbers do not include Crimea,
which has been annexed by Russia,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.072
or parts of the eastern provinces
of Donetsk and Luhansk,

00:00:43.191 --> 00:00:45.536
which are not currently
under Ukrainian control.

00:00:45.961 --> 00:00:48.369
Those areas have a
Russian-speaking majority.

00:00:48.786 --> 00:00:52.643
It's also worth noting that most Ukrainian
speakers can also speak Russian,

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.559
even if it's as a second language.

00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:59.100
Some people may hear Ukrainians speaking
 Russian and think to themselves,

00:00:59.164 --> 00:01:01.711
"Wow, Ukrainian is so similar to Russian".

00:01:01.736 --> 00:01:03.692
But, what they should say to themselves is,

00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:07.110
"Wow, Russian is so similar to Russian".

00:01:07.135 --> 00:01:11.879
On top of that some people use a mixture of
Ukrainian and Russian called Surzhyk,

00:01:11.991 --> 00:01:14.061
and they may not be consciously aware of

00:01:14.086 --> 00:01:16.451
which elements are Ukrainian
and which are Russian.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.233
This code-mixing may also blur the
lines between Ukrainian and Russian.

00:01:20.685 --> 00:01:24.900
However, despite the sometimes unclear
delineation between the two languages,

00:01:24.925 --> 00:01:28.270
Standard Russian and Standard Ukrainian
are clearly distinct,

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.920
and the vernacular speech of most
people is also clearly distinct.

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:35.519
Both languages developed from
the Old East Slavic language,

00:01:35.544 --> 00:01:38.648
which comprised a set of
mutually intelligible dialects

00:01:38.853 --> 00:01:40.723
spoken within the Federation of Rus,

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:43.747
which later came to be
referred to as Kievan Rus.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.430
These Old East Slavic
speakers are thought of

00:01:46.455 --> 00:01:50.093
as the ancestors of today's
Russians, Ukrainians, and Belarusians.

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:55.211
When Kievan Rus fell, the Slavic tribes
were divided into two separate states,

00:01:55.295 --> 00:01:58.375
and the Old East Slavic dialects
in those separate states

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.790
began to grow apart from each other
and undergo different influences.

00:02:02.456 --> 00:02:06.314
Tribes to the east came under the
rule of the Grand Duchy of Moscow,

00:02:06.339 --> 00:02:08.458
and their dialects evolved into Russian.

00:02:08.836 --> 00:02:11.180
Standard Russian, which is dominant today,

00:02:11.205 --> 00:02:14.151
developed with more influence
from Old Church Slavonic,

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:18.270
the old Slavic literary language which
differed from vernacular dialects.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.560
Also, under Peter the Great,

00:02:20.585 --> 00:02:23.655
who aimed to turn Russia into a
major Western power.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.687
Russian adopted a significant
amount of vocabulary

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:28.609
from French and other European languages.

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:32.363
Tribes to the west came under
the Grand Duchy of Lithuania,

00:02:32.449 --> 00:02:34.807
and then the Polish-Lithuanian
Commonwealth.

00:02:34.998 --> 00:02:37.837
Standard Ukrainian was based
on spoken vernacular

00:02:37.964 --> 00:02:40.125
specifically the southeastern dialects,

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.686
and lacks much of the Old Church
Slavonic influence that Russian has.

00:02:44.662 --> 00:02:47.628
Ukrainian also underwent
a lot of Polish influence

00:02:47.653 --> 00:02:51.041
due to geographic proximity
and Polish political control,

00:02:51.066 --> 00:02:53.866
with Polish being a lingua franca
for a period of time.

00:02:54.088 --> 00:02:56.912
This is especially true in
Western Ukrainian dialects.

00:02:57.419 --> 00:03:00.912
Now, let's cut to the chase and start
looking at the languages themselves.

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:02.229
Vocabulary.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:06.467
Russian and Ukrainian share
about 62% lexical similarity.

00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:09.089
I personally found this
number surprisingly low.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.872
Ukrainian has a higher lexical similarity

00:03:11.897 --> 00:03:14.853
with Polish, Slovak and Belarusian,
than it does with Russian,

00:03:14.878 --> 00:03:16.242
especially with Belarusian.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.401
Even among very basic words
there are lots of differences.

00:03:20.028 --> 00:03:21.409
The word for paper.

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:22.904
In Ukrainian: papir,

00:03:23.266 --> 00:03:25.083
and in Russian: bumaga.

00:03:25.353 --> 00:03:28.321
I suspect the Ukrainian word
was borrowed from Polish,

00:03:28.346 --> 00:03:30.000
which in turn borrowed it from German.

00:03:30.282 --> 00:03:33.682
The word for sugar:
tsukor - sakhar

00:03:34.119 --> 00:03:37.905
Both of these words are cognates that
trace back to Proto-Indo-European.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.533
The Russian word traces
back to Old East Slavic,

00:03:40.558 --> 00:03:42.904
while the Ukrainian word
was borrowed from Polish,

00:03:42.929 --> 00:03:44.247
which borrowed it from German.

00:03:44.847 --> 00:03:47.966
The word for money: hroshi - den'gi.

00:03:48.521 --> 00:03:51.347
This Russian word is a borrowing
from a Turkic language,

00:03:51.581 --> 00:03:55.564
but Russian also has a cognate
of the Ukrainian word: groshi,

00:03:55.588 --> 00:03:58.255
which refers to pennies or
a small amount of money,

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.052
and is also the name of
some older types of coins.

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:04.647
The word for mourning:
ranok - utro

00:04:05.015 --> 00:04:06.904
The word for bottle: plyashka,

00:04:07.212 --> 00:04:09.611
this word traces back to
Polish and then to German,

00:04:09.856 --> 00:04:13.577
butylka, this Russian word is
also a loanword from Polish

00:04:13.602 --> 00:04:15.467
which in turn got it from French.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.568
In Polish, "butelka" is
a more general word,

00:04:18.593 --> 00:04:21.052
 while "flaszka" is more for hard liquor.

00:04:22.062 --> 00:04:23.945
Also, the names of months are different.

00:04:24.191 --> 00:04:27.879
In Russian, the names of months came
from Latin via Old Church Slavonic.

00:04:28.114 --> 00:04:32.142
In Ukrainian, the names of the months
are original vernacular Slavic words.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.352
For example, January.
In Russian : yanvar'

00:04:35.734 --> 00:04:37.694
and in Ukrainian: sichen'

00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:42.171
and February:
fevral' - lyutyy, and so on

00:04:43.144 --> 00:04:45.624
As with most languages
which are closely related,

00:04:45.649 --> 00:04:48.167
there are lots of false friends
in Russian and Ukrainian.

00:04:48.395 --> 00:04:51.101
Words that appear to have the same meaning,
but actually don't.

00:04:51.126 --> 00:04:54.014
"chas", this Ukrainian word means time.

00:04:54.268 --> 00:04:57.037
"chas", this Russian word means hour.

00:04:57.323 --> 00:04:59.680
"harbuz", this means pumpkin.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.508
"arbuz", and this means watermelon.

00:05:02.838 --> 00:05:05.218
"nedilya", this means Sunday.

00:05:05.402 --> 00:05:07.711
"nedelya", and this means week.

00:05:08.128 --> 00:05:11.860
"svaryty", and this means to scold
in the imperfective form.

00:05:12.016 --> 00:05:15.475
"svarit'", and this means to boil
in the perfective form

00:05:15.945 --> 00:05:18.199
"svit", this word means world.

00:05:18.224 --> 00:05:20.192
"svet", this means light.

00:05:20.442 --> 00:05:23.485
This Russian word does have a
secondary meaning of "world",

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:25.442
but only in some fixed expressions.

00:05:26.382 --> 00:05:28.731
"layaty", this means to scold

00:05:28.787 --> 00:05:31.175
"layat'", this word means to bark.

00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:33.785
Orthography and pronunciation.

00:05:34.008 --> 00:05:37.158
Both languages use varieties
of the Cyrillic alphabet,

00:05:37.183 --> 00:05:38.833
but there are a number of differences

00:05:38.858 --> 00:05:41.548
between the Russian variety
and the Ukrainian variety.

00:05:42.004 --> 00:05:46.154
First, Ukrainian has this letter,
which represents the sound g,

00:05:46.274 --> 00:05:48.051
 but Russian doesn't have this letter.

00:05:48.213 --> 00:05:50.978
In Russian g is represented by this letter,

00:05:51.185 --> 00:05:54.613
In Ukrainian, this letter is
normally pronounced as h.

00:05:55.115 --> 00:05:59.019
Next, Ukrainian has this letter
which represents the sound i,

00:05:59.226 --> 00:06:01.050
but Russian doesn't have this letter.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:03.671
In Russian this letter
represents the sound i,

00:06:04.306 --> 00:06:07.615
Ukrainian has this letter which
represents the sound ye.

00:06:07.866 --> 00:06:11.056
In Russian this letter represents
the similar sound ye.

00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:14.405
But, in Ukrainian this letter
represents the sound e,

00:06:14.747 --> 00:06:18.254
and in Russian that sound e
is represented by this letter,

00:06:18.279 --> 00:06:20.413
a letter which isn't used in Ukrainian.

00:06:21.409 --> 00:06:24.729
Russian has this letter which
represents the sound e,

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.808
a letter which Ukrainian doesn't have.

00:06:27.025 --> 00:06:30.410
Instead, in Ukrainian this letter
represents the equivalent sound,

00:06:30.435 --> 00:06:32.199
in addition to the sound e.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.346
For example, the
words meaning "soap",

00:06:34.489 --> 00:06:38.506
in Russian "mylo"
and in Ukranian "mylo".

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.821
Ukrainian has this letter
to represent the sound yi

00:06:42.846 --> 00:06:44.598
but Russian doesn't have this letter.

00:06:44.788 --> 00:06:48.305
In Russian, this sound is represented
by a combination of two letters.

00:06:49.399 --> 00:06:52.249
Russian has this letter
to represent the sound yo,

00:06:52.447 --> 00:06:54.320
but Ukrainian doesn't use this letter,

00:06:54.376 --> 00:06:56.907
instead it uses a
combination of two letters.

00:06:57.479 --> 00:07:00.733
Russian has this special letter
that doesn't represent a sound,

00:07:00.758 --> 00:07:04.519
but indicates that the previous consonant
is in it's hard consonant form

00:07:04.718 --> 00:07:07.726
in a context where you would
expect the soft consonant form.

00:07:08.193 --> 00:07:09.876
Ukrainian doesn't use this letter,

00:07:09.901 --> 00:07:12.685
but instead uses an apostrophe
 after the consonant.

00:07:12.874 --> 00:07:15.532
So, in Russian,
it's written like this "ob"yekt",

00:07:15.826 --> 00:07:19.096
while in Ukrainian,
it's written like this "ob"yekt".

00:07:19.453 --> 00:07:20.453
Grammar.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.453
The concepts of grammar, in Russian and
Ukrainian, are generally the same.

00:07:24.823 --> 00:07:28.070
They both have three genders
masculine, feminine, and neuter.

00:07:28.249 --> 00:07:31.022
They both have almost
the same set of noun cases,

00:07:31.047 --> 00:07:33.266
that apply to adjectives
and pronouns, as well.

00:07:33.511 --> 00:07:36.636
They share the same major
verb conjugations, and so on.

00:07:36.743 --> 00:07:39.162
But, the exact word forms
are often different,

00:07:39.187 --> 00:07:41.120
or at least not entirely the same.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:43.080
Let's look at the different case forms

00:07:43.105 --> 00:07:45.549
for the noun meaning
"head"  in both languages.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:46.962
These words are cognates.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.040
In Russian: golova,
and in Ukrainian: holova

00:07:51.472 --> 00:07:53.715
These are both in the
nominative singular form.

00:07:53.882 --> 00:07:55.872
Notice that in the nominative singular,

00:07:55.897 --> 00:07:57.508
they are spelled exactly the same,

00:07:57.533 --> 00:07:58.786
but sound fairly different.

00:07:59.112 --> 00:08:01.594
The first consonant is
one that I mentioned before,

00:08:01.619 --> 00:08:03.967
which normally sounds like g in Russian,

00:08:03.992 --> 00:08:06.070
but h in Ukrainian.

00:08:06.763 --> 00:08:11.809
In Russian, the o vowel is frequently
reduced to a or swha a when unstressed,

00:08:11.834 --> 00:08:14.364
while in Ukrainian it
tends to not be reduced.

00:08:14.626 --> 00:08:16.315
Now, looking at the case forms,

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:19.727
one thing to notice is that the case
endings have some different vowels.

00:08:20.008 --> 00:08:22.872
Some are different letters
representing equivalent sounds.

00:08:23.174 --> 00:08:26.356
For example, the Russian
genitive singular "golovy",

00:08:26.491 --> 00:08:29.554
and the Ukrainian
genitive singular, "holovy".

00:08:30.013 --> 00:08:31.813
Others represent different sounds.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.782
For example, the Russian
dative singular "golove",

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:37.370
and Ukrainian dative singular "holovi".

00:08:37.505 --> 00:08:40.866
Another thing to notice is that
while Ukrainian has a vocative case,

00:08:40.891 --> 00:08:41.981
Russian does not

00:08:42.244 --> 00:08:45.211
The vocative form is the form
you use when you are addressing

00:08:45.236 --> 00:08:46.649
or calling someone or something.

00:08:47.171 --> 00:08:49.829
The old vocative form
was lost from Russian,

00:08:50.042 --> 00:08:51.899
except for a few specific words.

00:08:51.924 --> 00:08:53.916
For example, when addressing God.

00:08:53.988 --> 00:08:54.972
Bozhe.

00:08:55.734 --> 00:08:59.344
It seems that it's being used less and
less in spoken Ukrainian, as well.

00:08:59.598 --> 00:09:02.606
But, it's still used by some and
is part of standard Ukrainian.

00:09:03.612 --> 00:09:07.412
In Russian, a new vocative form
is apparently starting to develop,

00:09:07.437 --> 00:09:09.325
in which the final vowel is dropped,

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.694
usually from names and familial words.

00:09:11.837 --> 00:09:14.805
For example, "mama" becomes "mam".

00:09:15.173 --> 00:09:18.861
But, whether or not it's a genuine
vocative case form is debated.

00:09:20.362 --> 00:09:23.901
Another common difference is that
in Russian, the copula - byt'-

00:09:24.107 --> 00:09:27.091
the word meaning "to be" is
not used in the present tense

00:09:27.369 --> 00:09:29.010
This is often true in Ukrainian too,

00:09:29.035 --> 00:09:31.216
which has a lot of dialectal variation.

00:09:31.412 --> 00:09:35.422
But, in formal standard speech,
and in western dialects closer to Poland,

00:09:35.456 --> 00:09:37.542
the copula is commonly used.

00:09:37.567 --> 00:09:41.098
For example, let's look at the
sentences meaning "this is illegal".

00:09:41.416 --> 00:09:43.574
In Russian: eto nezakonno.

00:09:44.138 --> 00:09:47.114
In Ukrainian, it could be similar:
tse nezakonno.

00:09:47.376 --> 00:09:49.336
Or, it might have the copula like this:

00:09:49.518 --> 00:09:50.749
tse ye nezakonno

00:09:51.122 --> 00:09:54.764
In western Ukraine, it might be like this:
to ye nezakonno

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:58.422
"to ye" is similar to "to jest" in Polish.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.562
Another difference is that Ukrainian
has some additional verb conjugations,

00:10:03.587 --> 00:10:04.983
that Russian doesn't have.

00:10:05.271 --> 00:10:09.158
In Ukrainian, there's an additional
way to express the imperfective future

00:10:09.183 --> 00:10:10.374
that Russian doesn't have.

00:10:10.557 --> 00:10:12.682
Take the sentence meaning
"they will be eating"

00:10:12.707 --> 00:10:15.151
or "they will eat" in an
imperfective tense.

00:10:15.311 --> 00:10:17.524
In Russian: Oni budut yest'.

00:10:17.651 --> 00:10:20.635
And now in Ukrainian, first
with an equivalent structure:

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.111
Vony budut' yisty.

00:10:22.638 --> 00:10:26.228
In both languages, there's the subject
pronoun, the third-person plural,

00:10:26.253 --> 00:10:29.201
then the future conjugation
of the verb meaning "to be",

00:10:29.248 --> 00:10:31.827
followed by the infinitive
of the lexical verb,

00:10:31.954 --> 00:10:34.040
the main verb that supplies
the core meaning.

00:10:34.152 --> 00:10:37.366
But, Ukrainian also has
this form: Vony yistymut'.

00:10:37.678 --> 00:10:39.534
rather than using
the verb "to be"

00:10:39.559 --> 00:10:42.513
a suffix is attached
to the infinitive,

00:10:42.538 --> 00:10:46.512
followed by the same future inflection
that appeared on the verb meaning "to be".

00:10:46.726 --> 00:10:51.069
This alternate conjugation is called the
imperfective synthetic future tense.

00:10:51.989 --> 00:10:55.765
Ukrainian also has a pluperfect
conjugation that Russian doesn't have,

00:10:55.894 --> 00:10:58.495
though not everyone uses
it in Ukrainian anymore.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:01.029
For example, here's a sentence meaning:

00:11:01.054 --> 00:11:04.321
"I had been reading this book,
but I forgot its contents".

00:11:04.346 --> 00:11:06.726
Ya chytav buv tsyu knyzhku,
ta zabuv yiyi zmist.

00:11:07.417 --> 00:11:09.012
The pluperfect is formed

00:11:09.037 --> 00:11:11.788
with the past tense perfective
form of the lexical verb,

00:11:11.813 --> 00:11:14.424
followed by the past tense of
the verb meaning "to be".

00:11:14.843 --> 00:11:16.586
In Russian, the sentence would be:

00:11:16.683 --> 00:11:20.341
Ya chital etu knigu no
zabyl yeyo soderzhaniye.

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:24.054
In Russian, the past tense
perfective form of the verb is used,

00:11:24.079 --> 00:11:26.089
rather than a special pluperfect form.

00:11:26.833 --> 00:11:29.097
This might be a good time
to stop for a moment,

00:11:29.122 --> 00:11:32.155
and examine some other differences
between these two sentences,

00:11:32.298 --> 00:11:35.028
Before, we saw that the words
for "this" are different,

00:11:35.258 --> 00:11:37.313
that was in the neuter nominative form.

00:11:37.385 --> 00:11:39.956
Here they are, both in the
feminine accusative form,

00:11:40.265 --> 00:11:42.432
and again they take on
slightly different forms.

00:11:42.653 --> 00:11:45.468
Next, notice the words
meaning book are cognate,

00:11:45.493 --> 00:11:46.493
but with different forms.

00:11:46.792 --> 00:11:49.135
The Ukrainian word is
in its diminutive form,

00:11:49.142 --> 00:11:50.056
knyzhku,

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:51.975
which is the everyday way to say book.

00:11:52.316 --> 00:11:55.040
It can also be said with
the same form as in Russian,

00:11:55.211 --> 00:11:57.280
but that would be more formal in Ukrainian.

00:11:57.407 --> 00:11:59.988
In Russian, there is
also a diminutive form,

00:12:00.146 --> 00:12:01.146
kishka,

00:12:01.209 --> 00:12:03.060
it's less common than in Ukrainian,

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:06.575
and is even more informal word for
a book or something like a booklet.

00:12:07.025 --> 00:12:09.455
Both words are in the
accusative singular form here.

00:12:09.805 --> 00:12:12.167
Notice that the words for
"but" are different.

00:12:12.424 --> 00:12:16.708
However, it's worthnoting that the primary
meaning of the Ukrainian word is "and".

00:12:16.733 --> 00:12:19.939
and this word "ale" is a
more common word for "but".

00:12:20.665 --> 00:12:23.005
And, notice the cognate
words meaning "forgot"

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:24.625
that takes somewhat different forms.

00:12:25.109 --> 00:12:29.504
"ee" these are the third-person feminine
singular genitive personal pronouns,

00:12:29.529 --> 00:12:31.020
and these words are cognates.

00:12:31.384 --> 00:12:34.187
And finally, we see the nouns
meaning content.

00:12:34.289 --> 00:12:38.614
The Russian word "soderzhaniye"
consists of the verb "soderzat"

00:12:38.815 --> 00:12:44.354
meaning "to contain" plus "anije" a suffix
that forms abstract nouns from verbs.

00:12:44.708 --> 00:12:48.550
The Ukrainian word is a simple noun
that isn't composed of these two parts.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:52.585
Another difference is that Russian,
at least formal Russian,

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:54.600
makes use of active
participles

00:12:54.625 --> 00:12:57.727
to give extra information 
about a noun or a noun phrase,

00:12:57.752 --> 00:12:59.224
while Ukrainian does not.

00:12:59.249 --> 00:13:02.690
For example, the phrases meaning
"the woman sitting on the chair".

00:13:02.888 --> 00:13:06.245
First, in Russian:
zhenshchina sidyashchaya na stule.

00:13:06.617 --> 00:13:08.728
This phrase has an active participant.

00:13:08.753 --> 00:13:12.594
But, in Ukrainian this meaning would
be expressed using a relative clause

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:14.729
zhinka, shcho sydyt' ba stil'tsi.

00:13:14.956 --> 00:13:17.657
This is like saying,
"the woman that sits on the chair".

00:13:18.697 --> 00:13:22.458
That's also how this meeting would be
expressed in daily speech in Russian

00:13:22.665 --> 00:13:25.371
zhenshchina, kotoraya sidit na stule.

00:13:25.518 --> 00:13:28.724
In a Russian sentence,
the relative pronoun is "kotoraya"

00:13:28.873 --> 00:13:31.260
But, in Ukrainian there
are several possibilities,

00:13:31.392 --> 00:13:36.544
"kotryy", which is cognate with the
Russian word "yakyy" or "shcho",

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:38.254
as in the sentence above.

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:42.589
And, as a side note, look at the different
forms the word for woman takes.

00:13:42.684 --> 00:13:44.345
In Russian, it's "zhenshchina",

00:13:44.573 --> 00:13:46.858
while in Ukranian it's "zhinka".

00:13:47.382 --> 00:13:49.468
The preposition meaning "on" is the same,

00:13:49.493 --> 00:13:51.676
except for slightly
different vowel quality.

00:13:51.866 --> 00:13:55.471
And, the word for "chair" are clearly
related, but take on different forms.

00:13:55.543 --> 00:13:58.223
In the most basic form,
the nominative singular,

00:13:58.248 --> 00:14:00.200
the Russian word is "stule"

00:14:00.515 --> 00:14:02.758
and the Ukrainian
word is "stil'tsi".

00:14:03.525 --> 00:14:05.437
Here they are in the locative case,

00:14:05.462 --> 00:14:08.953
though for Russian, it's often referred
to as the prepositional case.

00:14:09.858 --> 00:14:13.251
Now, let's look at one final
sentence and see what we find.

00:14:13.616 --> 00:14:16.984
These sentences mean 'I can't go
because I'll be working tomorrow'.

00:14:17.159 --> 00:14:21.595
In Russian: Ya ne mogu poyti,
potomu chto zavtra (ya) budu rabotat'.

00:14:21.865 --> 00:14:25.840
And in Ukrainian: Ya ne mozhu pity,
tomu shcho zavtra (ya) budu pratsyuvaty.

00:14:25.865 --> 00:14:28.111
Word for word, both sentences are:

00:14:28.136 --> 00:14:34.366
I-negative-can-go-because-
tomorrow-I-will be-to work.

00:14:35.088 --> 00:14:37.655
Notice that the first
two words meaning 'I',

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.695
then the 'negative'
are basically the same.

00:14:40.064 --> 00:14:42.300
Next, the words meaning 'can' are cognates,

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:44.794
but have grown apart and
are now pronounced differently,

00:14:44.926 --> 00:14:46.712
notably the second consonant.

00:14:46.841 --> 00:14:49.211
Next, the verbs meaning 'go' are cognates,

00:14:49.299 --> 00:14:51.593
they are both perfective infinitive forms.

00:14:51.779 --> 00:14:55.956
Both verbs contain a perfective prefix
attached to the imperfective verb.

00:14:56.750 --> 00:15:00.805
Next, there's a compound conjunction
meaning 'because' in both languages,

00:15:01.027 --> 00:15:02.599
and these parts are cognates.

00:15:02.716 --> 00:15:05.328
But, the Russian compound
contains an extra element.

00:15:05.828 --> 00:15:09.812
In Ukranian, a different conjunction
"bo" can also be used.

00:15:10.196 --> 00:15:13.166
Next, the words for 'tomorrow'
are basically the same.

00:15:13.688 --> 00:15:16.204
After that,
we see the subject pronoun again,

00:15:16.229 --> 00:15:18.659
but it isn't necessary and can be dropped,

00:15:18.684 --> 00:15:21.858
because the subject is clear from
the conjugation of the verb.

00:15:21.883 --> 00:15:24.906
Both Russian and Ukrainian
are pro-drop languages

00:15:25.198 --> 00:15:28.631
Next, the imperfective future tense
is formed in the same way

00:15:28.789 --> 00:15:32.280
with the verb meaning 'to be' as the
auxiliary verb in the future  tense,

00:15:32.305 --> 00:15:35.566
followed by the infinitive of the
lexical verb meaning 'to work'.

00:15:35.802 --> 00:15:38.057
But, the lexical verbs
themselves are different,

00:15:38.217 --> 00:15:41.002
the Ukrainian word was
borrowed from Polish.

00:15:41.683 --> 00:15:44.545
Now, remember that Ukrainian
has the alternate way

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:46.793
of saying something in
the imperfective future.

00:15:50.625 --> 00:15:51.990
As I'm sure you can see,

00:15:52.015 --> 00:15:55.016
Russian and Ukrainian are very
closely related languages

00:15:55.041 --> 00:15:56.657
 that share a lot of similarities

00:15:56.780 --> 00:16:00.772
But, they also have a lot of differences,
some of them minor and some of them major.

00:16:00.907 --> 00:16:04.922
Lots of different vocabulary, different
pronunciation, and different word forms,

00:16:04.947 --> 00:16:07.437
that have arisen over time
to make the two languages

00:16:07.462 --> 00:16:09.460
only partially mutually intelligible

00:16:09.485 --> 00:16:12.175
to people who don't have much
exposure to the other language.

00:16:12.299 --> 00:16:15.249
But, since most Ukrainian
speakers also speak Russian

00:16:15.274 --> 00:16:17.211
or have extensive exposure to it,

00:16:17.236 --> 00:16:19.465
asymmetric intelligibility is the norm.

00:16:19.738 --> 00:16:22.887
In other words, Ukrainian speakers
 can often understand Russian,

00:16:22.912 --> 00:16:25.580
while the Russian speaker
doesn't understand Ukrainian,

00:16:25.605 --> 00:16:28.229
especially Russian speakers
from outside Ukraine.

00:16:28.503 --> 00:16:31.661
The question of the day.
For speakers of Russian and Ukrainian:

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:34.169
How similar do you think
the two languages are?

00:16:34.375 --> 00:16:36.526
To what extent can you
understand the other one?

00:16:36.822 --> 00:16:37.995
And for other people:

00:16:38.121 --> 00:16:40.866
How similar do Russian and
Ukrainian seem to you?

00:16:41.022 --> 00:16:42.403
If you enjoyed this video,

00:16:42.428 --> 00:16:45.681
be sure to check out Langfocus on
 the various social media platforms

00:16:45.706 --> 00:16:48.196
like Facebook, YouTube,
Instagram, and Twitter.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:52.390
And, as always, I want to give a very
